<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Success statistics and corresponding heuristic value baselines for example reasoning prefixes. <math id="A3.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{exp}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{exp}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{pes}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>pes</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{pes}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are derived from observed success rates within the prefix’s subtree, reflecting different assumptions of future success.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C Staged Advantage Estimation: A Minimal Example ‣ Tree-OPO: Off-policy Monte Carlo Tree-Guided Advantage Optimization for Multistep Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> demonstrates staged advantage computation. The <span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expectation baseline</span> (<math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math>) best satisfies the <em id="A3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tree consistency constraints</em>. Its accurate reflection of expected rewards ensures advantages consistently signal better/worse-than-expected outcomes relative to a path’s true potential. This yields informative, context-sensitive advantages. In contrast, <math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{pes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>pes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{pes}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s coarse assignments lead to misleading signals, failing tree consistency.</p>
</div>
